# Hello mdVOXI

This is the simplest possible mdVOXI document. It combines regular
markdown text with embedded VOXI visualizations.

## A Simple Graphic

Here's an inline card rendered right in the document:

~~~voxi
CANVAS 400 120 #1a1d27
RECT 10 10 380 100 1 #232733 8
TEXT 30 40 "Hello, mdVOXI!" 24 2 #6c8cff bold
TEXT 30 72 "Markdown meets visual computing" 12 2 #8888a0
~~~

## A Bar Chart

Programming languages I use, by hours per week:

~~~chart bar
"Weekly Hours" = Python:18 JavaScript:12 Go:8 Rust:4 SQL:6
colors = #e53935 #fdd835 #00bcd4 #ff9800 #43a047
~~~

## A 3D Scene

And here's a tiny voxel penguin sitting right in the notes:

~~~voxi3d 35 25
V 16 20 12
-- Body
E 8 6 6 3 6 3 0
-- Belly
E 8 6 4 2 5 1 8
-- Head
S 8 14 6 2 0
-- Eyes
P 7 15 4 8
P 9 15 4 8
-- Beak
B 7 14 2 2 1 2 7
-- Feet
B 5 0 5 2 1 2 7
B 10 0 5 2 1 2 7
~~~

## Regular Markdown Still Works

- **Bold text** works fine
- *Italic text* too
- `Inline code` as expected
- [Links](https://example.com) work normally

> [!tip] The beauty of mdVOXI is that it degrades gracefully.
> In any standard markdown viewer, the VOXI blocks just appear as
> fenced code blocks — still readable, just not rendered visually.

---

*Written in mdVOXI v0.1*
